<h2 class="mt-14">Consent and purchaser suppression for Customer service</h2>
<p>Customer Service readiness starts with provable consent records and instant post-checkout suppression across each app running customer service sends. Customer Service audiences forgive plain creative; they do not forgive promos sent mid-refund or right after checkout.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Audience state</th><th>Required handling</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>No documented consent</td><td>No customer service promos without proof; transactional messages only</td><td>Without customer service consent proof, marketing has no legal footing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consented, never purchased</td><td>Lead customer service newcomers with teaching and social proof</td><td>Premature customer service offers teach shoppers to wait</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active cart, no checkout</td><td>Customer Service cart reminder first; earn the discount decision</td><td>Margin-safe customer service recovery at peak intent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchased recently</td><td>Customer Service buyers get education, not another pitch</td><td>Customer Service suppression protects the new-customer glow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refund or return open</td><td>Customer Service selling waits for case resolution</td><td>Open customer service cases reset what the shopper tolerates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeated non-engagement</td><td>Remove chronic customer service non-openers pre-complaint</td><td>Shields customer service sender reputation and inboxing</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS consent present</td><td>Hold customer service texts to quiet hours and frequency limits</td><td>Customer Service SMS complaints cost more per incident</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wholesale or B2B account</td><td>Isolate customer service wholesale comms from retail blasts</td><td>B2B customer service mis-sends risk contract breach</td></tr>
<tr><td>Free or disposable email domain</td><td>Screen customer service free/disposable emails before flows</td><td>Customer Service bounces from junk signups hurt inboxing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff and test accounts</td><td>Customer Service internal accounts never enter production</td><td>Customer Service reporting stays clean without test noise</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competitor or researcher signals</td><td>Treat customer service snoops under normal consent policy</td><td>Customer Service one-offs breed audit gaps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy list without timestamps</td><td>Reconfirm customer service consent where timestamps are missing</td><td>Customer Service lists without proof invite liability</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="mt-12">Margin, app costs, and pricing for Customer service</h2>
<p>Judge customer service on margin, not attribution. A durable customer service program absorbs subscription, overages, SMS, capture, creative, agency, and discount costs and still pays. Customer Service rule: near fifteen percent stack-to-margin, cut customer service complexity before chasing gains.</p>
<p>Pricing changes frequently and varies by region, contact volume, and contract term, so check the official pricing pages of every shortlisted app and model an eighteen-month total that includes a peak season. Customer Service free tiers look generous until one limit bites — verify which of contacts, sends, branching, or support constrains customer service first.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost component</th><th>What to model</th><th>Common failure</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Platform subscription</td><td>Plan tier sized to actual customer service list trajectory</td><td>Customer Service tier bought for phantom growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contact or send overages</td><td>customer service expansion rate tested on plan caps</td><td>Unmodeled customer service peaks triggering invoices</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS credits</td><td>Scale customer service SMS by opt-ins and journey length</td><td>Treating customer service texts like near-free email</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discount budget</td><td>Customer Service discount load from depth and uptake</td><td>customer service flows teaching shoppers to hold out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Creative and ops time</td><td>Weekly customer service hours to run and QA flows</td><td>customer service maintenance hours wished away</td></tr>
<tr><td>Migration and setup</td><td>Customer Service import, consent mapping, flow rebuild</td><td>Customer Service consent lost mid-migration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support and success tiers</td><td>Customer Service severity coverage: paid or included</td><td>Customer Service support gaps found at peak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third-party integrations</td><td>Adjacent customer service tooling: reviews, loyalty, capture</td><td>Customer Service sprawl doubling real platform cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deliverability remediation</td><td>customer service deliverability upkeep and guidance cost</td><td>Customer Service reputation repair dwarfing license fees</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
